const e={note:"The Arabic esoteric sciences are the least known to an English-speaking reader and the most often misrepresented, so three cautions govern this hall. First, their standing in Islam was contested and remains so: the letter-sciences, talismanic magic and alchemy were practised in the great madrasas and condemned by serious jurists, sometimes in the same city and the same generation, and the currents present neither the practice nor the condemnation as the mainstream. Second, attribution is difficult in exactly the way it is in Latin: the Jābirian corpus is the work of many hands over more than a century, and the Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm was ascribed to al-Majrīṭī for centuries on grounds now generally rejected. Third, this is a transmission story as much as a tradition: a great deal of what Latin Europe called magic and alchemy reached it through these books, in translation, and the vocabulary of both still carries the Arabic.",themes:[{slug:"ilm-al-huruf",title:"The Science of Letters",native:"علم الحروف",translit:"ʿilm al-ḥurūf",summary:"Twenty-eight letters, each with a number, an element and a rank in creation — the same arithmetic as Hebrew gematria, and by inheritance rather than by borrowing.",paragraphs:["The arithmetic comes first. Under the old abjad order — memorised as the eight nonsense-words abjad hawwaz ḥuṭṭī kalaman saʿfaṣ qarashat thakhadh ḍaẓagh — every Arabic letter carries a value, alif one through ghayn a thousand, and the reckoning of a word's total is called ḥisāb al-jummal. The order itself is the evidence of descent: the first twenty-two letters run in the sequence of the Northwest Semitic parent alphabet, aleph-beth-gimel-daleth, so that Arabic bāʾ is two exactly as Hebrew bet is two; and the six letters Arabic added for sounds the parent did not write — thāʾ, khāʾ, dhāl, ḍād, ẓāʾ, ghayn — are appended at the end with the leftover values five hundred to a thousand. Arabic lettrism and Hebrew gematria are therefore not two systems, one copied from the other, but one inheritance worked in two languages, with Greek isopsephy a third cousin of the same Hellenistic habit. The Maghribī abjad of North Africa and al-Andalus recites ṣaʿfaḍ qarisat thakhadh ẓaghush instead of the Mashriqī saʿfaṣ qarashat thakhadh ḍaẓagh, moving the values of six letters, which changes the sum of a great many words and is the first thing to establish before any figure is trusted.","Twenty-nine sūras of the Qurʾān open with disconnected letters — alif lām mīm at Q 2:1, kāf hā yā ʿayn ṣād at Q 19:1, ḥā mīm followed by ʿayn sīn qāf across Q 42:1–2 — and the fourteen distinct letters used, exactly half the alphabet, are known in the later literature as the luminous letters, al-ḥurūf al-nūrāniyya. These muqaṭṭaʿāt are the standing invitation to the whole science, because the tradition has never agreed what they are. Al-Ṭabarī, in the Jāmiʿ al-bayān on Q 2:1, canvasses a dozen incompatible explanations — abbreviations of divine names, oaths, the names of the sūras, letters whose meaning God alone knows — and among them reports the story of Medinan Jews computing alif-lām-mīm as seventy-one and reading in it the duration of the new community, a report he sets out without endorsing. Most exegetes rejected the numerical reading. That it was available to be rejected in the ninth century tells us how old the habit is.","The systematising runs from several directions at once. Sahl al-Tustarī (d. 283/896) reads the letters as the substance of the divine speech; the Andalusian Ibn Masarra of Cordoba (d. 319/931) left a Kitāb khawāṣṣ al-ḥurūf, lost until Muḥammad Kamāl Ibrāhīm Jaʿfar published it in 1978, in which the letters are the intelligible structure of creation; the Jābirian corpus binds the letters of a substance's name to the proportion of natures within it; and Shīʿī and Ismāʿīlī speculation on the muqaṭṭaʿāt supplies much of the vocabulary. Ibn al-ʿArabī gives the second chapter of al-Futūḥāt al-Makkiyya to the science of letters, treating them as a community with its own prophets and saints, and their vowels and points as the ranks within it. The name under which all this became a discipline is Aḥmad al-Būnī (d. 622/1225 or 630/1232–3), an emigrant from the Maghrib teaching at Cairo, who distributed the divine names among the letters and built the awfāq, the magic squares, on their values.","What the manuals describe can be set out without being taught. Basṭ spells a letter out into its full name, so that alif becomes alif-lām-fāʾ and acquires a second, larger number. Taksīr breaks a name into its letters and recombines them. The twenty-eight are dealt round the four elements in repeating fours — alif fire, bāʾ air, jīm water, dāl earth, and so on — and separately assigned to the seven planets and the twenty-eight lunar mansions, a fit the tradition treats as a proof rather than a coincidence, since the letters and the mansions are the same number. On these values are built the awfāq, of which the smallest and most reproduced is the three-by-three square summing to fifteen in every direction, whose four corner cells hold two, four, six and eight: read as letters, bāʾ dāl wāw ḥāʾ, which is why buduḥ appears on amulets from Morocco to Java as though it were the name of a spirit. Chronogram verse, in which a line's letters sum to the year of the event it commemorates, is the same arithmetic in ordinary literary use.","The legal position was never settled, and the sources should be read for the argument rather than for a verdict. Ibn Khaldūn, in Book VI of the Muqaddima, separates sīmiyāʾ, the letter-science, from siḥr proper, treats it as a late outgrowth of Sufism, grants that its operations work, and classes it among the sciences the Law forbids — a position that concedes efficacy and withholds permission in the same breath. Ibn Taymiyya condemned it outright. Against that, lettrism was court business: Timurid and Ottoman chanceries employed men who practised it, and Matthew Melvin-Koushki has argued at length that the fifteenth and sixteenth centuries were an occult renaissance rather than a decadence. The Ḥurūfiyya of Faḍl Allāh Astarābādī, executed in 796/1394, pushed the science to its limit by reading the human face as an alphabet — though its scriptures are Persian, and belong to that hall. Noah Gardiner's work on the manuscripts has shown that the printed Shams al-maʿārif al-kubrā is a much later compilation than the book al-Būnī wrote, which means a large part of what is confidently said about medieval lettrism rests on a nineteenth-century lithograph."],terms:[{native:"حساب الجمل",translit:"ḥisāb al-jummal",gloss:"the abjad reckoning: the summing of a word by the numerical values of its letters. The same arithmetic as Hebrew gematria and Greek isopsephy, and by shared Semitic inheritance rather than by borrowing"},{native:"الحروف المقطعة",translit:"al-ḥurūf al-muqaṭṭaʿa",gloss:"the disconnected letters opening twenty-nine sūras; fourteen distinct letters, exactly half the alphabet, called in the later literature the luminous letters"},{native:"بسط",translit:"basṭ",gloss:"expansion: spelling a letter out into its full written name, so that alif yields alif-lām-fāʾ and a second, larger number"},{native:"تكسير",translit:"taksīr",gloss:"breaking: the decomposition of a name into its letters and their recombination into new words and figures"},{native:"وفق / أوفاق",translit:"wafq / awfāq",gloss:"a magic square, and the plural; built on letter-values, and the characteristic device of the Būnian literature"},{native:"بدوح",translit:"buduḥ",gloss:"the letters bāʾ, dāl, wāw, ḥāʾ — two, four, six, eight — the corner cells of the three-by-three square, treated on amulets as though the word were a spirit's name"},{native:"سيمياء",translit:"sīmiyāʾ",gloss:"letter-magic; from Greek sēmeia, signs. Ibn Khaldūn uses it as the name of the discipline and distinguishes it from siḥr"}],texts:[{title:"al-Ṭabarī, Jāmiʿ al-bayān, on Q 2:1",note:"A dozen incompatible explanations of the disconnected letters, gathered in the ninth century without a decision between them — including the numerical reading the commentator does not endorse."},{title:"Ibn al-ʿArabī, al-Futūḥāt al-Makkiyya, chapter 2",note:"The letters as a community with its own ranks, prophets and saints; the fullest integration of lettrism into a metaphysics rather than into a technique."},{title:"Aḥmad al-Būnī, Shams al-maʿārif",note:"The classical handbook — but read the early, short recension in manuscript, not the vast Shams al-maʿārif al-kubrā lithographed at Cairo, which Noah Gardiner has shown to be a much later compilation."},{title:"Ibn Khaldūn, Muqaddima, Book VI",note:"The chapters on sorcery and talismans and on the secrets of letters: efficacy granted, legitimacy refused, and the science placed historically as a late offshoot of Sufism."},{title:"Ibn Masarra, Kitāb khawāṣṣ al-ḥurūf",note:"A tenth-century Andalusian treatise on the properties of letters, unknown until published by Muḥammad Kamāl Ibrāhīm Jaʿfar in 1978; studied by Sarah Stroumsa and Sara Sviri."}]},{slug:"the-most-beautiful-names",title:"The Most Beautiful Names",native:"الأسماء الحسنى",translit:"al-asmāʾ al-ḥusnā",summary:"A hadith says ninety-nine, a hundred less one — and then two canonical lists that do not agree, and a hidden name that is not on either.",paragraphs:["The Qurʾān gives the principle without the list. To God belong the most beautiful names, says Q 7:180, so call upon Him by them; Q 17:110 and Q 20:8 repeat the phrase, and the closing verses of Q 59:22–24 run a sequence of some seventeen names together in a passage that reads like a litany already. The number ninety-nine comes from a hadith transmitted in both Bukhārī and Muslim: God has ninety-nine names, a hundred less one, and whoever encompasses them enters the Garden. The verb is aḥṣā, which means to count, to number, to take full account of — and the whole later argument turns on whether it means recitation or something considerably more demanding. What neither Bukhārī nor Muslim supplies is the enumeration. That comes in a version transmitted by al-Tirmidhī in his Sunan, and in a different version in Ibn Māja, and the two lists do not match.","This is the fact most often lost. The ninety-nine names printed on posters, engraved on plaques and sold on prayer-bead cards are al-Tirmidhī's list, which the hadith critics have generally judged a transmitter's insertion rather than the Prophet's own words, and which several major scholars declined to treat as fixed. Al-Bayhaqī gathered the alternatives in al-Asmāʾ wa-l-ṣifāt. The theological question behind the enumeration is older and sharper still: are the names tawqīfī, licensed only by revelation and not to be extended by inference, or may a name be derived from an act God performs in scripture? And are the attributes the names denote identical with the divine essence, as the Muʿtazila held, or additional to it, as the Ashʿarīs held — a dispute that occupied Islamic theology for three centuries and was never resolved by anything but the exhaustion of one party.","Al-Ghazālī's al-Maqṣad al-asnā, written in the years around his withdrawal from Baghdad, takes the practical question head-on and answers it in a way that shaped everything after. To encompass the names is not to enumerate them but to acquire them: the human being is to take on, within the limits of a creature, the trait each name denotes — takhalluq, the assumption of character. To know al-Raḥmān is to become merciful; to know al-Ṣabūr is to become patient. He is careful about the limit, insisting throughout that the resemblance is one of trace and not of kind, and equally careful to say that the list of ninety-nine is not necessarily closed. Ibn al-ʿArabī pushes the same doctrine into cosmology: the names are the relations under which an unknowable Essence turns towards creation, they have no reality apart from it, and every existing thing is governed by one name as its particular lord.","In practice the names are worked rather than contemplated. They are told on the subḥa, the string of thirty-three or ninety-nine beads whose use is itself contested by those who prefer counting on the fingers; the tasbīḥ ascribed to Fāṭima distributes thirty-three, thirty-three and thirty-four repetitions after the prayers; and the orders assign particular names as awrād, apportioned by a shaykh to a disciple according to what that disciple's state is judged to need — al-Laṭīf for constriction, al-Ḥayy for lethargy, and so through the list. The lettrist literature goes further and computes: each name has an abjad value, and the Būnian manuals prescribe repetitions numbering that value, at hours governed by the planet assigned to the name's dominant letter. Al-Ghazālī would have recognised the first of these practices and not the second, which is roughly where the boundary between devotion and operation was drawn, and redrawn.","Then there is the one that is not on the list. Al-ism al-aʿẓam, the greatest name, is held in a body of hadith — the reports are in Abū Dāwūd, al-Tirmidhī and Ibn Māja — to be a name by which God, when asked, gives. The reports describe men praying and the Prophet identifying that they have used it, but they do not agree on which name it was, and the tradition divides three ways: that it stands among the ninety-nine and is concealed within them, as Friday is concealed within the week; that it is a particular combination or a phrase such as al-Ḥayy al-Qayyūm or Dhū l-Jalāl wa-l-Ikrām; or that it is unknown, and the search for it is a distraction. The third position is held by sober theologians and the first two by nearly everyone else, and the search for the greatest name is the standing motive of a very large part of Arabic lettrist and talismanic writing — the point at which a devotional discipline and an operative science turn out to be pursuing the same object."],terms:[{native:"الأسماء الحسنى",translit:"al-asmāʾ al-ḥusnā",gloss:"the most beautiful names; the Qurʾānic phrase (Q 7:180, 17:110, 20:8) that the ninety-nine lists set out to fill in"},{native:"إحصاء",translit:"iḥṣāʾ",gloss:"encompassing, taking full account of; the verb in the hadith of the ninety-nine, and the word on which the whole argument about recitation versus realisation turns"},{native:"تخلق",translit:"takhalluq",gloss:"the assumption of a divine trait as human character; al-Ghazālī's answer to what encompassing the names actually requires"},{native:"توقيفي",translit:"tawqīfī",gloss:"fixed by revelation and not open to extension by reasoning; the position that a name of God may only be used if scripture uses it"},{native:"الاسم الأعظم",translit:"al-ism al-aʿẓam",gloss:"the greatest name; held to compel an answer, disputed as to whether it is one of the ninety-nine, a combination of them, or unknown"},{native:"سبحة",translit:"subḥa",gloss:"the prayer-string of thirty-three or ninety-nine beads; also called misbaḥa, and objected to by those who hold that the fingers are the sunna"}],texts:[{title:"Ṣaḥīḥ al-Bukhārī and Ṣaḥīḥ Muslim, the hadith of the ninety-nine names",note:"'A hundred less one, and whoever encompasses them enters the Garden.' Both collections give the number and the promise; neither gives the list."},{title:"al-Tirmidhī, Sunan, Kitāb al-Daʿawāt",note:"The enumeration everyone uses. A different enumeration appears in Ibn Māja, and the two do not agree — which is why the printed ninety-nine is a tradition rather than a text."},{title:"al-Ghazālī, al-Maqṣad al-asnā fī sharḥ maʿānī asmāʾ Allāh al-ḥusnā",note:"The classic commentary, arguing that the names are to be acquired as character rather than recited as a list, and that ninety-nine is not necessarily a closed number. Translated by David Burrell and Nazih Daher (1992)."},{title:"al-Bayhaqī, al-Asmāʾ wa-l-ṣifāt",note:"The eleventh-century gathering of the variant lists and of the theological dispute over whether the attributes are the essence or additional to it."},{title:"Ibn Barrajān of Seville, Sharḥ asmāʾ Allāh al-ḥusnā",note:"A twelfth-century Andalusian commentary read closely by Ibn al-ʿArabī's milieu; the names as the structure through which an unknowable Essence is addressed."}]},{slug:"stations-and-states",title:"Stations and States",native:"المقامات والأحوال",translit:"al-maqāmāt wa-l-aḥwāl",summary:"Sufism as a technical psychology with a syllabus: what you earn, what you are given, and a hundred stages between repentance and the end.",paragraphs:["The word taṣawwuf most probably derives from ṣūf, wool, for the coarse garment ascetics wore — the derivation from ṣafāʾ, purity, is a pious etymology the sources themselves offer and the philologists reject. What the word names is not a mood but a curriculum, and by the tenth century it had a technical literature designed to teach it. Abū Naṣr al-Sarrāj (d. 378/988) fixed the framework in the Kitāb al-Lumaʿ, edited by R. A. Nicholson in 1914: seven maqāmāt or stations — repentance, scrupulousness, renunciation, poverty, patience, trust, contentment — and ten aḥwāl or states, among them vigilance, nearness, love, fear, hope, longing, intimacy and certainty. Al-Kalābādhī's Kitāb al-Taʿarruf and Abū Ṭālib al-Makkī's Qūt al-qulūb belong to the same generation and the same project, which was to show that this discipline had a doctrine, a vocabulary and a defensible place inside Sunnī Islam.","The distinction between the two lists is the whole of the technique, and al-Qushayrī states it in a formula: the states are gifts and the stations are earnings. A maqām is achieved by effort, is held, and is not lost; one does not leave the station of repentance behind on arriving at the station of patience but stands in both. A ḥāl descends unbidden, may last an instant, and cannot be produced by any exertion — it is precisely what the traveller does not control. Much of the literature's practical advice concerns the mismanagement of states: the disciple who takes a passing condition for a permanent attainment, who mistakes the sweetness of an early experience for arrival, or who cultivates states for their own sake, which the manuals treat as one of the reliable ways to ruin the work.","Al-Qushayrī's Risāla, written at Nishapur in 437/1045, is the book on which the respectability of the discipline was built. It opens with biographies of some eighty-three earlier masters, precisely to establish that these were pious men of the community and not a sect, and then gives some fifty chapters on the technical terms, each defined, illustrated from the sayings and anchored to a Qurʾānic verse and a hadith. ʿAbd Allāh al-Anṣārī of Herat (d. 481/1089) did the opposite in the Manāzil al-sāʾirīn: a hundred stations in ten sections of ten, each defined in a few dense lines, ascending from awakening to the end, with each station itself divided into three degrees for the common, the elect and the elect of the elect. It became the standard syllabus, and its commentators include ʿAbd al-Razzāq al-Kāshānī on the Akbarian side and — a fact worth pausing over — Ibn Qayyim al-Jawziyya, the pupil of Ibn Taymiyya, whose three-volume Madārij al-sālikīn is a Ḥanbalī commentary on a Sufi manual.","Beneath the stations lies a psychology of the self. The Qurʾān grades the nafs from the soul that commands to evil (Q 12:53), through the soul that reproaches itself (Q 75:2), to the soul at rest (Q 89:27), and the whole tradition builds its account of progress on those three verses. Al-Muḥāsibī of Baghdad (d. 243/857) — the surname means one who takes account of himself — gave the discipline its first sustained method in al-Riʿāya li-ḥuqūq Allāh: a continuous audit of one's own motives, on the assumption that self-deception is not an occasional failure but the normal condition of the soul, and that the ego's most reliable manoeuvre is to disguise itself as piety. Later writers add the subtle centres, the laṭāʾif, located in the body and awakened in order; the Naqshbandī and Kubrawī systems are the most fully articulated, and they differ from each other in both the number and the placing.","None of this is a museum. The orders that teach these stages are living institutions with millions of adherents: the Qādiriyya, from ʿAbd al-Qādir al-Jīlānī of Baghdad (d. 561/1166), the most widely diffused of all; the Shādhiliyya, from Abū al-Ḥasan al-Shādhilī (d. 656/1258), whose Egyptian and Maghribī branches are numerous; the Naqshbandiyya across Central and South Asia and Turkey; the Tijāniyya and Murīdiyya across West Africa; the Chishtiyya in South Asia. In Egypt the orders are regulated by statute under a Supreme Council of Sufi Orders. They are also under pressure: Turkey closed the lodges by law in 1925 and has not reopened them as such, Salafi movements reject the whole apparatus of shaykh, chain and litany, and members of Sufi congregations have been attacked and killed in Egypt, Pakistan and elsewhere within the last decade. What is described above is a historical account of a discipline that people are practising this week."],terms:[{native:"مقام / مقامات",translit:"maqām / maqāmāt",gloss:"station; a stage attained by effort and permanently held. Al-Sarrāj lists seven, al-Anṣārī a hundred"},{native:"حال / أحوال",translit:"ḥāl / aḥwāl",gloss:"state; a condition given rather than earned, which may pass in an instant. Al-Qushayrī's formula: the states are gifts, the stations are earnings"},{native:"تصوف",translit:"taṣawwuf",gloss:"the discipline itself; most probably from ṣūf, wool, for the ascetic's garment. The pious derivation from ṣafāʾ, purity, is rejected by the philologists"},{native:"محاسبة",translit:"muḥāsaba",gloss:"self-audit, the taking of account against oneself; the method of al-Muḥāsibī, who took his surname from it"},{native:"ذوق",translit:"dhawq",gloss:"tasting; direct experiential knowledge, set against what is learnt by report or demonstration. Al-Ghazālī makes it the criterion of certainty"},{native:"صحو وسكر",translit:"ṣaḥw wa-sukr",gloss:"sobriety and intoxication; the two temperaments the tradition contrasts in Junayd of Baghdad and in Abū Yazīd al-Bisṭāmī, and the terms of a long argument about which is the higher condition"}],texts:[{title:"Abū Naṣr al-Sarrāj, Kitāb al-Lumaʿ",note:"Tenth century; the framework of seven stations and ten states, and the first systematic defence of the discipline as orthodox. Edited by R. A. Nicholson, 1914."},{title:"al-Qushayrī, al-Risāla al-Qushayriyya",note:"Written at Nishapur in 437/1045. Eighty-three master-biographies to establish the pedigree, then some fifty chapters defining the technical vocabulary — the standard reference of the tradition."},{title:"ʿAbd Allāh al-Anṣārī, Manāzil al-sāʾirīn",note:"A hundred stations in ten sections of ten, each with three degrees; the compressed syllabus on which the later commentary literature was built."},{title:"Ibn Qayyim al-Jawziyya, Madārij al-sālikīn",note:"A vast Ḥanbalī commentary on the Manāzil by a pupil of Ibn Taymiyya — evidence that the line between Sufism and its critics ran through individual books rather than between camps."},{title:"al-Ḥārith al-Muḥāsibī, al-Riʿāya li-ḥuqūq Allāh",note:"Ninth-century Baghdad; the method of continuous self-examination, premised on the assumption that piety is the ego's preferred disguise."}]},{slug:"dhikr-and-sama",title:"Remembrance and Audition",native:"الذكر والسماع",translit:"al-dhikr wa-l-samāʿ",summary:"The one practice every order shares, the one that has divided them for a thousand years, and the fatwas on both.",paragraphs:["Dhikr is Qurʾānic before it is Sufi. Remember God with much remembrance, says Q 33:41; in the remembrance of God do hearts find rest, says Q 13:28. What the orders built on those verses is a technology of repetition. The formula may be the shahāda, or the single name Allāh, or the pronoun huwa, or one of the ninety-nine; the count may be a hundred, a thousand, or the abjad value of the name; the session may be solitary or a congregation of hundreds. The central division is between dhikr jahrī, uttered aloud, and dhikr khafī, held in the heart without sound, and it is not a matter of taste but of lineage: the Naqshbandiyya took silent dhikr as a defining principle, tracing it through Abū Bakr, while the orders that trace themselves through ʿAlī more often practise the loud form. The Shādhilī master Ibn ʿAṭāʾ Allāh al-Iskandarī (d. 709/1309) wrote one of the few sustained manuals of the practice, Miftāḥ al-falāḥ, describing the coordination of formula with breath and the order in which the subtle centres are addressed.","The collective session, the ḥaḍra, has a shape that can be described from the outside: the recitation of the order's own litany, the wird or ḥizb transmitted from its founder; the invocation taken up in unison and gradually accelerated; movement, standing or swaying or turning, entering as the tempo rises; and sometimes wajd, a state of ecstasy that may issue in cries or collapse. The manuals distinguish wajd, which comes, from tawājud, its deliberate cultivation, and are divided on whether the second is legitimate preparation or fraud. Al-Ghazālī, who defends the practice, is careful that the litany is licit and the state is not manufactured. What the Sufi writers themselves say about the danger is worth registering: the states are the least important part, they are describing a means, and a disciple who chases them has misunderstood the exercise.","Samāʿ — listening, audition — is where the argument becomes legal. The word covers the recitation of poetry, chanted verse and, in many settings, instruments; it is the ground of the qawwālī of the Chishtī shrines of South Asia, of the Mawlawī turning ceremony, of the inshād of Egyptian mawlids. Al-Ghazālī devoted the eighteenth book of the Iḥyāʾ ʿulūm al-dīn, on the etiquette of audition and ecstasy, to defending it under conditions: the hearer's state, the company, the occasion and the content of what is sung all bear on whether a given session is permitted, and the same act may be lawful for one man and forbidden for another. Ibn al-Jawzī answered him in Talbīs Iblīs, attacking the whole practice as a device of Satan dressed as devotion; Ibn Taymiyya issued fatwas against ritualised dance and against samāʿ with instruments — while himself, on George Makdisi's much-debated reading of the sources, holding an affiliation in the Qādirī chain.","The variety is greater than the argument suggests. The Mawlawī samāʿ, organised as a formal rite by Rūmī's son Sulṭān Walad after his father's death in 1273, is a choreographed ceremony with a fixed sequence of movements, an order of musical modes and a costume whose parts are given a symbolic reading. The Rifāʿiyya became notorious for ordeals performed in the state of wajd — Ibn Baṭṭūṭa, visiting their centre at Umm ʿUbayda in Iraq in the 1320s, describes dervishes entering fire, and his account is a traveller's report of a spectacle rather than a doctrinal source. The Naqshbandiyya, at the other pole, hold the silent dhikr against all of it and their manuals regard audible ecstasy with open suspicion. Khalwa, solitary retreat of forty days, gives the Khalwatiyya its name and belongs to the same family of disciplines, worked alone.","All of this continues, and the disputes continue with it. The Turkish Republic closed the tekkes and lodges by law in November 1925, which made the Mawlawī ceremony illegal as a rite; it returned from 1953 as a public performance at Konya, and the ceremony was proclaimed by UNESCO in 2005 and inscribed on its representative list in 2008 — an outcome in which a devotional practice survives by being classified as heritage, and about which the order's own members do not agree. Qawwālī fills the Chishtī shrines of Delhi and Ajmer weekly. Egyptian mawlids draw crowds in the hundreds of thousands. Against this, Salafi criticism of collective vocal dhikr, of instruments and of shrine festivals is sustained and well organised, and in Saudi Arabia and elsewhere such gatherings have been restricted or forbidden. Neither the practice nor the objection is a historical matter."],terms:[{native:"ذكر",translit:"dhikr",gloss:"remembrance; the repeated invocation of a name or formula, and the practice common to every order. Qurʾānic before it is technical (Q 33:41, Q 13:28)"},{native:"جهري وخفي",translit:"jahrī wa-khafī",gloss:"uttered aloud and hidden; the two modes of dhikr, and a marker of lineage rather than of preference — the Naqshbandiyya are defined by the silent form"},{native:"حضرة",translit:"ḥaḍra",gloss:"presence; the collective dhikr session, and by extension the assembly itself"},{native:"ورد / حزب",translit:"wird / ḥizb",gloss:"the litany transmitted from an order's founder and assigned to a disciple; the Shādhilī Ḥizb al-baḥr is the best known"},{native:"وجد وتواجد",translit:"wajd wa-tawājud",gloss:"ecstasy and its deliberate cultivation; the manuals separate the two and disagree over whether the second is legitimate preparation"},{native:"سماع",translit:"samāʿ",gloss:"audition; listening to chant, poetry and in many settings instruments as a spiritual exercise. The most litigated practice in the whole tradition"}],texts:[{title:"al-Ghazālī, Iḥyāʾ ʿulūm al-dīn, Book 18: on the etiquette of audition and ecstasy",note:"The defence that made samāʿ arguable within orthodoxy — conditional, hedged, and holding that the same act may be lawful for one hearer and forbidden for another. Translated by Duncan Macdonald in the Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society, 1901–2."},{title:"Ibn al-Jawzī, Talbīs Iblīs",note:"The twelfth-century Ḥanbalī counter-attack: the whole apparatus of session, audition and ecstasy read as a deception. Still the reference point for the objection."},{title:"Ibn ʿAṭāʾ Allāh al-Iskandarī, Miftāḥ al-falāḥ wa-miṣbāḥ al-arwāḥ",note:"A Shādhilī manual of dhikr, unusual in describing the practice technically — formula, breath, posture, and the order of the subtle centres."},{title:"Ibn Baṭṭūṭa, Riḥla",note:"An eyewitness of the Rifāʿī ordeals at Umm ʿUbayda in the 1320s; a traveller's account of a spectacle, and to be weighed as such rather than as doctrine."},{title:"Ibn Taymiyya, fatwas on samāʿ and dance",note:"The legal case against ritualised audition, gathered in the Majmūʿ al-fatāwā; to be read alongside George Makdisi's argument that its author stood within a Qādirī chain, which is contested."}]},{slug:"ibn-arabi-and-the-imaginal",title:"Ibn ʿArabī and the Imaginal",native:"عالم المثال",translit:"ʿālam al-mithāl",summary:"An intermediate world where spirits take form and bodies grow subtle — and a doctrine of one Being that has been called the summit of Islamic metaphysics and plain unbelief, continuously, for seven hundred years.",paragraphs:["Muḥyī al-Dīn Ibn al-ʿArabī was born at Murcia on 17 Ramaḍān 560 / 28 July 1165 and died at Damascus on 28 Rabīʿ II 638 / 16 November 1240, and left the largest body of mystical prose in Islam: al-Futūḥāt al-Makkiyya in five hundred and sixty chapters, and the hundred-page Fuṣūṣ al-ḥikam, twenty-seven chapters each named for a prophet in whom one wisdom of God is set as a stone is set in a ring. Osman Yahia's inventory of 1964 counted some eight hundred and fifty titles circulating under his name and later scholarship has judged a large share of them misattributed. His central terms became the working vocabulary of everything after him: the fixed entities, the breath of the Merciful, the perfect man, the divine names as the real articulation of the world, and self-disclosure — tajallī — as a succession of appearances that never repeats, so that the cosmos is renewed at every instant and no two moments of it are the same.","The barzakh is the hinge of the system. The word is Qurʾānic, naming the barrier between the two seas at Q 25:53 and the interval between death and resurrection at Q 23:100, and Ibn al-ʿArabī generalises it into the technical name for anything that both joins and separates two orders — the line between sun and shade that belongs to neither, the imagination between sense and intellect. From it follows the doctrine of an intermediate world where spirits are given form and bodies become subtle: the place in which visions, dream-figures, the forms of the dead and the appearances of angels are actually located, and are neither hallucinations nor material objects. Ibn al-ʿArabī's usual name for it is the world of imagination, ʿālam al-khayāl; the phrase ʿālam al-mithāl belongs more to Suhrawardī and to the school that came after both of them, and the two vocabularies are often run together in modern accounts.","The step that made him permanently controversial is elsewhere. If there is only one Being, and everything other than God is a determination of it — a self-disclosure, not a second thing existing alongside the first — then the distinction between Creator and creature has to be restated in terms that are not those of ordinary theology. Ibn al-ʿArabī never uses the phrase waḥdat al-wujūd. William Chittick has shown that the term is not his; it appears to have been fixed as the name of his doctrine by his followers and, in its polemical form, by Ibn Taymiyya, who used it as a charge. His son-in-law and foremost pupil Ṣadr al-Dīn al-Qūnawī, and after him al-Kāshānī, al-Qayṣarī, al-Jīlī and al-Nābulusī, built the system that goes by the name; the master himself wrote in a mode that the school found necessary to organise.","The controversy is real, continuous, and has not been settled by anyone. Ibn Taymiyya (d. 728/1328) declared the doctrine unbelief. In Mamluk Cairo the quarrel became a public affair when Ibrāhīm al-Biqāʿī circulated a Tanbīh al-ghabī ilā takfīr Ibn ʿArabī — an alerting of the ignorant to the excommunication of Ibn ʿArabī — and al-Suyūṭī answered with a Tanbīh al-ghabī bi-tabriʾat Ibn ʿArabī, alerting the same ignorant to his acquittal, the two titles differing by a word. Selim I, on taking Damascus in 1518, had the tomb restored and a mosque built over it, which settled the Ottoman position for four centuries. In Mughal India the Naqshbandī Aḥmad Sirhindī (d. 1034/1624) proposed waḥdat al-shuhūd, unity of witnessing, as a corrective: the unity is in the perception of the one who sees, not in being itself — an argument conducted largely in Persian, and still the standard alternative. Today the Fuṣūṣ is taught with commentary in Iranian seminaries as part of the ʿirfān curriculum and denounced in Salafi literature as heresy, and both are current positions.","The Western reception has its own distortions, and they are worth naming because most English-language readers arrive through them. Henry Corbin coined mundus imaginalis, and in English imaginal, in an essay of 1964, specifically so that the tradition's claims about this intermediate order would not be filed under the imaginary; the coinage is exact and useful, and the Islam it belongs to is a highly selected one — Shīʿī, Iranian, Ishrāqī, read phenomenologically and largely outside history, and criticised for that ever since. Corbin's Ibn ʿArabī, in L'imagination créatrice dans le soufisme d'Ibn ʿArabî (1958), is a reading and not a translation. The scholarly foundation in English is Chittick's, whose method is to translate long stretches of the Futūḥāt and let the vocabulary establish itself. The Muhyiddin Ibn ʿArabi Society, founded in 1977, has published a journal on the corpus for nearly fifty years. The distinction between what Ibn al-ʿArabī wrote and what has been made of him is one every reader in this hall has to keep making."],terms:[{native:"برزخ",translit:"barzakh",gloss:"isthmus; a barrier that joins as it separates. Qurʾānic (Q 25:53, Q 23:100), and generalised by Ibn al-ʿArabī into the name for every intermediate degree of being"},{native:"عالم الخيال",translit:"ʿālam al-khayāl",gloss:"the world of imagination; Ibn al-ʿArabī's more usual term for the intermediate order that later writers and modern accounts call ʿālam al-mithāl"},{native:"الأعيان الثابتة",translit:"al-aʿyān al-thābita",gloss:"the fixed entities; the eternal realities of things in God's knowledge, which determine what each thing can receive — so that, in his formulation, God decrees upon a thing only what the thing's own reality decrees upon itself"},{native:"نفس الرحمن",translit:"nafas al-Raḥmān",gloss:"the breath of the Merciful; the divine outbreathing in which the words of the cosmos are articulated, so that existence is speech and the world is a sentence"},{native:"وحدة الوجود",translit:"waḥdat al-wujūd",gloss:"unity of being; the name of the doctrine, used by his school and by his accusers but not by him. William Chittick has established that the phrase is not Ibn al-ʿArabī's own"},{native:"وحدة الشهود",translit:"waḥdat al-shuhūd",gloss:"unity of witnessing; Aḥmad Sirhindī's proposed correction — that the oneness lies in the perceiver's experience rather than in being — still the standard alternative"}],texts:[{title:"Ibn al-ʿArabī, al-Futūḥāt al-Makkiyya",note:"Five hundred and sixty chapters, begun at Mecca about 598/1202, with a first recension in 629/1231 and a second in 636/1238. The complete metaphysics, and the place where the imaginal is worked out at length."},{title:"Ibn al-ʿArabī, Fuṣūṣ al-ḥikam",note:"Twenty-seven chapters, each a prophet as a bezel for one wisdom. Its preface states that the Prophet handed the author the book in a dream at Damascus in Muḥarram 627 / December 1229 — the tradition's account of the text is the author's own opening sentence."},{title:"Ibrāhīm al-Biqāʿī, Tanbīh al-ghabī ilā takfīr Ibn ʿArabī; al-Suyūṭī, Tanbīh al-ghabī bi-tabriʾat Ibn ʿArabī",note:"The Mamluk quarrel in its purest form: two treatises whose titles differ by one word, one excommunicating and one acquitting the same man."},{title:"William C. Chittick, The Sufi Path of Knowledge (1989) and The Self-Disclosure of God (1998)",note:"The foundation of serious English-language work: long translated stretches of the Futūḥāt rather than paraphrase, and the demonstration that waḥdat al-wujūd is not Ibn al-ʿArabī's phrase."},{title:"Henry Corbin, 'Mundus imaginalis, ou l'imaginaire et l'imaginal' (1964)",note:"Where the English word imaginal comes from. An exact coinage inside a highly selective reading of Islam, and the door through which most Anglophone readers arrive."}]},{slug:"arabic-alchemy",title:"Alchemy in Arabic",native:"الكيمياء",translit:"al-kīmiyāʾ",summary:"Three thousand titles under one name that may not correspond to a person, a balance computed from the letters of a substance's name, and the vocabulary English still uses.",paragraphs:["The word itself is contested. Arabic al-kīmiyāʾ comes from Greek chēmeia, and the Greek is derived either from cheō, to pour or to fuse, or from the Egyptian kmt, the black land, in which case the art is named for Egypt; neither derivation has prevailed, and the older confident assertions of the Egyptian one are no longer safe. What is certain is the transmission. Alchemical texts came into Arabic in the same ninth-century movement that brought Galen and Ptolemy, and one of the earliest and strangest is Sirr al-khalīqa, written in the voice of Balīnās — Apollonius of Tyana — who reports finding, in a vault beneath a statue of Hermes, an old man holding a tablet of emerald. The dozen sentences on that tablet, beginning with the correspondence of what is above to what is below, have no Greek original and surface first here, in Arabic. Julius Ruska placed the compilation in the reign of al-Maʾmūn (813–833); Paul Kraus and, following the manuscripts, Ursula Weisser placed it earlier, about 750–800.","The largest single body of alchemical writing produced anywhere before the modern period passes under the name of Jābir ibn Ḥayyān: the Hundred and Twelve Books, the Seventy Books, the Books of the Balances, some three thousand titles in all. The tradition makes him a Kūfan of the eighth century, pupil of the sixth Shīʿī imām Jaʿfar al-Ṣādiq, and the corpus says so on nearly every page. Ibn al-Nadīm, compiling the Fihrist in 377/987, already records people arguing over whether he existed. Paul Kraus, in two volumes of 1942–3, showed from the corpus's dependence on translations made after Jābir's supposed death, from its technical vocabulary and from its Ismāʿīlī doctrinal signature that it is the work of a school writing between roughly 850 and 950. Fuat Sezgin defended an authentic eighth-century kernel in 1971 and Syed Nomanul Haq renewed the case in 1994; the kernel is genuinely open, the dating of the bulk is not seriously disputed.","The Jābirian mīzān is what makes this corpus belong in a hall about letters. Every substance holds the four natures — hot, cold, moist, dry — in a hidden proportion; that proportion can be computed; and it is computed from the letters of the substance's Arabic name, distributed by their abjad values across the four natures in a series Kraus reconstructed as one, three, five and eight, summing to seventeen, with its multiples governing the degrees. Alchemy thereby becomes a numerical science of the correspondence between speech and matter, and the operation on a metal and the operation on a name are the same operation. The corpus also carries the sulphur–mercury theory of metals, in which all metals are compounds of the same two principles in differing purity — the doctrine that makes transmutation intelligible rather than miraculous — and, in the Book of Gathering, a procedure for takwīn, the artificial generation of living beings.","The other tradition is flatly practical. Abū Bakr al-Rāzī (c. 251/854 – 313/925 or 320/932), hospital director at Rayy and Baghdad and the greatest clinician of the age, wrote the Kitāb al-Asrār with a classification of substances into spirits, bodies, stones, vitriols, boraxes and salts, an inventory of apparatus, and procedures written to be followed rather than decoded — no balance-numerology, almost no mystagogy. Ibn Umayl (d. c. 350/960) went the other way, reading the art as a body of allegory to be interpreted, and his al-Māʾ al-waraqī reached Latin as the Tabula chemica under the name Senior Zadith. Against all of them stood serious opposition from inside Islamic learning: al-Kindī wrote a treatise refuting the alchemists' claim; Ibn Sīnā, in the mineralogy of the Kitāb al-Shifāʾ, denied that species can be transmuted, and that passage, translated into Latin and appended to Aristotle's Meteorologica, became the standard European authority against alchemy for three hundred years; Ibn Khaldūn rejected the art on the ground that no adept has ever completed what all of them describe.","The transmission into Latin is unusually well dated. The Liber de compositione alchemiae, in which the monk Morienus instructs the Umayyad prince Khālid ibn Yazīd, carries a preface dating the translation to 1144 and names the translator Robert of Chester, usually identified with Robert of Ketton; it is conventionally the first alchemical text in Latin. What crossed with the texts was the vocabulary, and English still speaks it: alchemy from al-kīmiyāʾ, elixir from al-iksīr and behind it Greek xērion, a wound-powder; alembic from al-anbīq and behind it Greek ambix; alkali from al-qily, the ash of saltwort; borax from būraq; realgar from rahj al-ghār, powder of the mine; aludel from al-uthāl; tutty from tūtiyā; and alcohol from al-kuḥl, the antimony powder used on the eyes, whose sense shifted first to any fine sublimate and then to the spirit of wine. Athanor is usually derived from al-tannūr, the oven, though the alternative derivation from Greek athanatos, deathless, for the ever-burning furnace, is still repeated. The most influential Latin 'Geber', the Summa perfectionis, has no Arabic original at all: William Newman showed it to be the work of Paul of Taranto, a Franciscan of the late thirteenth century."],terms:[{native:"ميزان",translit:"mīzān",gloss:"balance; the Jābirian doctrine that a substance's proportion of the four natures can be computed from the letters of its name — the point at which alchemy and lettrism are one science"},{native:"إكسير",translit:"iksīr",gloss:"elixir; the transmuting agent, from Greek xērion, a drying powder for wounds. In Sufi usage a figure for whatever perfects the soul"},{native:"كبريت وزئبق",translit:"kibrīt wa-zaʾbaq",gloss:"sulphur and mercury; the two principles of which all metals are held to be composed in differing purity — the theory that makes transmutation a matter of refinement rather than of miracle"},{native:"تكوين",translit:"takwīn",gloss:"generation, bringing into being; in the Jābirian Book of Gathering, the artificial production of living beings, described as the art's furthest reach"},{native:"أنبيق",translit:"anbīq",gloss:"the still-head, from Greek ambix; through al-anbīq it becomes the English alembic"},{native:"قلي",translit:"qily",gloss:"the ash of saltwort, a source of alkaline salts; al-qily gives English alkali"}],texts:[{title:"The Jābirian corpus",note:"The Hundred and Twelve Books, the Seventy Books, the Books of the Balances and the Book of Gathering. The tradition ascribes them to a Kūfan pupil of Jaʿfar al-Ṣādiq (d. 148/765); Paul Kraus's two volumes of 1942–3 place the bulk in a school writing about 850–950, against which Fuat Sezgin (1971) and Syed Nomanul Haq (1994) argue for an authentic early kernel."},{title:"Abū Bakr al-Rāzī, Kitāb al-Asrār",note:"Substances classified, apparatus inventoried, procedures written to be followed. Published in German by Julius Ruska in 1937; the counter-case to the allegorical tradition, by the age's foremost physician."},{title:"Sirr al-khalīqa, and the Emerald Tablet within it",note:"The earliest witness anywhere to the Tabula Smaragdina, which has no Greek original. Ruska placed the compilation under al-Maʾmūn (813–833); Kraus and Weisser about 750–800."},{title:"Ibn Sīnā, Kitāb al-Shifāʾ, on minerals",note:"The denial that species can be transmuted. Translated into Latin as De congelatione et conglutinatione lapidum, appended to Aristotle's Meteorologica, and cited against alchemy across medieval Europe under its opening words."},{title:"Ibn Umayl, al-Māʾ al-waraqī wa-l-arḍ al-najmiyya",note:"The Silvery Water and the Starry Earth, tenth century; the allegorical tradition at its most sustained, known in Latin as the Tabula chemica of Senior Zadith."}]},{slug:"astral-magic-and-talismans",title:"Astral Magic and the Talisman",native:"غاية الحكيم",translit:"Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm",summary:"The most systematic handbook of astral magic in Arabic, and the book that — translated in Castile as the Picatrix — gave the Renaissance most of what it thought it had recovered from Egypt.",paragraphs:["The premise of the whole literature is a physics rather than a religion. The celestial bodies act upon the sublunary world; that much was Aristotelian common ground and no jurist disputed it. The talismanic sciences add a claim about capture: that an appropriate material, worked into an appropriate form, inscribed with an appropriate figure, at a moment when the relevant heaven stands in the relevant configuration, will hold that influence and continue to exert it. Hence ikhtiyārāt, the elections — the astrological determination of the moment — and hence the two indispensable schemes, the thirty-six decans that divide the zodiac into ten-degree segments, each with an image described in words inherited ultimately from Hellenistic Egypt, and the twenty-eight manāzil al-qamar, the lunar mansions through which the moon passes in a sidereal month, whose number matching the letters of the alphabet was never treated by this literature as a coincidence.","Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm, the Goal of the Sage, is the fullest Arabic statement: four books on the making of talismans, the images of the decans and mansions, the natures and prayers proper to each planet, suffumigations, the properties of stones and plants, and the theory that holds it together. It contains the account of al-ṭibāʿ al-tāmm, the perfect nature — the celestial counterpart of a philosopher's own soul, which Hermes is said to have met and questioned, and whose four names the Latin gives as Meegius, Betzahuech, Vacdez and Nufeneguediz. It contains the description of a city of Hermes with a talismanic image set over each of its four gates, the Adocentyn of the Latin and al-Ashmūnayn of the Arabic. It draws on the Rasāʾil Ikhwān al-Ṣafāʾ, on the Jābirian corpus, on Ibn Waḥshiyya's Nabataean literature and on reports of the star-rites of the Ṣābians of Ḥarrān, and it argues throughout that this is the crown of the sciences rather than siḥr — which is what any straightforward reading of the sharīʿa would make of it.","Its authorship has moved by a century within living scholarship, and the reader should hold both accounts. Ibn Khaldūn names the astronomer Maslama al-Majrīṭī of Madrid (d. c. 398/1007) as the author, and that ascription stood for six hundred years; it is untenable on internal grounds and is no longer defended. Hellmut Ritter, who edited the Arabic in 1933, and Martin Plessner, who translated it with him in 1962, read the book's reference to the author's earlier Rutbat al-Ḥakīm as dating that work to 439–442 AH and so put the Ghāya in the 1050s, and a great deal of published scholarship still rests on that reading. Maribel Fierro argued in 1996 that the figures should be read 339–342/950–953 and the author identified as Maslama ibn Qāsim al-Qurṭubī (d. 353/964), traveller, traditionist and occultist; Godefroid de Callataÿ and Sébastien Moureau have built on her case, and it now prevails — prevails rather than is settled, and it carries the awkward consequence that the Ghāya would then quote the Rasāʾil earlier than the Rasāʾil are usually dated.","The Ghāya is not alone and should not be read as though it were. Thābit ibn Qurra, the Ṣābian of Ḥarrān who translated much of the Greek mathematics for the ʿAbbāsids, is credited with a treatise on talismanic images that reached Latin as De imaginibus. A treatise on stellar rays, De radiis, survives only in Latin under al-Kindī's name and argues that words, figures and desires act on the world by the same radiation as the stars; the ascription is generally accepted and the Arabic is lost, which is a real limitation on what can be said about it. Fakhr al-Dīn al-Rāzī (d. 606/1210), one of the major Ashʿarī theologians of the age, is credited with al-Sirr al-maktūm on astral magic, and his own biographers argued over whether he wrote it. Ibn Waḥshiyya's Nabataean Agriculture, presented as a translation from an ancient Babylonian original, supplied much of the ritual detail — and Maimonides used it in the Guide of the Perplexed III.29 as his evidence for what the ancient star-worshippers believed.","The crossing into Latin is the most consequential event in the history of European magic. At the court of Alfonso X of Castile, about 1256–8, the Ghāya was turned into Castilian, and from that version came the Latin Picatrix, edited by David Pingree in 1986; the name has never been satisfactorily explained. The Latin keeps the images and the operations, trims the philosophy, and loses the Arabic book's insistence on its own dignity, so that what arrives in Europe is a manual rather than a science. Ficino used it in the third book of De vita libri tres (1489) while declining to name it; Agrippa drew on it; men were investigated for owning copies. Much of what the Renaissance believed it had recovered from Egypt it had in fact received from Cordoba by way of Toledo. On the Arabic side the objects survive in quantity — engraved seals, magic-medicinal bowls of the kind catalogued by Emilie Savage-Smith, Ottoman talismanic shirts covered in Qurʾānic text and numbered squares — and amulet-making of this general kind continues today across the Muslim world, defended by some as licit ruqya and rejected by others as the shirk the Qurʾān forbids."],terms:[{native:"طلسم / طلسمات",translit:"ṭilasm / ṭilasmāt",gloss:"talisman; from Greek telesma, a consecrated object. Into Arabic, and from Arabic into every European language"},{native:"الطباع التام",translit:"al-ṭibāʿ al-tāmm",gloss:"the perfect nature; the celestial counterpart of the philosopher's own soul, which Hermes is said in the Ghāya to have met and questioned"},{native:"منازل القمر",translit:"manāzil al-qamar",gloss:"the twenty-eight lunar mansions; the moon's stations through a sidereal month — the same number as the letters of the alphabet, which this literature treats as significant"},{native:"اختيارات",translit:"ikhtiyārāt",gloss:"elections; the astrological choice of the moment at which an operation is to be performed, without which a talisman is held to be inert"},{native:"صابئة",translit:"Ṣābiʾa",gloss:"the Sabians; the pagans of Ḥarrān who, on a much-repeated report in Ibn al-Nadīm's Fihrist, took the Qurʾānic name (Q 2:62) to secure protected status. Their star-rites are the Ghāya's chief ritual source"},{native:"سحر",translit:"siḥr",gloss:"sorcery; the Qurʾānic term (Q 2:102) and the legal category that the talismanic authors spend their prefaces arguing their own subject does not fall under"}],texts:[{title:"Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm",note:"Four books of astral magic. Arabic edited by Hellmut Ritter (1933); German by Ritter and Martin Plessner (1962), who dated it to the 1050s. Maribel Fierro's redating to c. 950–3, with Maslama ibn Qāsim al-Qurṭubī as author, now prevails without being settled."},{title:"Picatrix: the Latin version",note:"Made from a Castilian translation prepared for Alfonso X about 1256–8. Critical edition by David Pingree (Warburg Institute, 1986). The book Ficino used in De vita III without naming it."},{title:"Ibn Waḥshiyya, al-Filāḥa al-nabaṭiyya",note:"The Nabataean Agriculture, presented as a translation from an ancient Babylonian original — a claim disputed since the Middle Ages. Maimonides treats it as evidence for ancient star-worship in the Guide of the Perplexed III.29."},{title:"Pseudo-al-Kindī, De radiis",note:"On stellar rays, and on words, figures and desire acting by the same radiation. Surviving only in Latin; the ascription is generally accepted but the Arabic is lost, which limits what can be argued from it."},{title:"Emilie Savage-Smith, Science, Tools and Magic (1997)",note:"The objects rather than the texts: engraved seals, magic-medicinal bowls, divinatory instruments, catalogued and described — the material record against which the manuals can be checked."}]},{slug:"ikhwan-al-safa",title:"The Brethren of Purity",native:"إخوان الصفا",translit:"Ikhwān al-Ṣafāʾ",summary:"Fifty-two anonymous epistles that carry a reader from arithmetic to salvation without a break, and close with one on magic.",paragraphs:["Somewhere in the tenth century, most probably at Baṣra, a group of men who never gave their names composed an encyclopaedia and circulated it. The Rasāʾil Ikhwān al-Ṣafāʾ wa-Khullān al-Wafāʾ — the epistles of the Brethren of Purity and the Friends of Fidelity — run to fifty-two treatises in four parts: fourteen on the mathematical and propaedeutic sciences, seventeen on the natural sciences and the body, ten on the soul and the intellect, eleven on theology and the revealed law. They are designed to be read in order, and the design is the argument: a reader who begins with arithmetic and proceeds without a break to metaphysics is meant to find that the ascent has no seam in it, because the world has none. A summarising Risāla al-Jāmiʿa was added later, and after it a Jāmiʿat al-Jāmiʿa, compressing the compression.","The metaphysics is Pythagorean and Neoplatonic and states itself plainly in the first epistle: the science of number is the root of the sciences, and number arises from the one as beings arise from the Creator. Above the world stand the Universal Intellect and the Universal Soul; the cosmos is a single living body whose parts are in sympathy; the human being is the small copy of it; and the soul's descent and return is the plot of everything. That first epistle on arithmetic also contains, incidentally, some of the earliest recorded magic squares in Arabic, from three by three upwards — a detail that shows how short the distance is in this literature between a mathematical curiosity and an operative device. The epistle on music treats intervals as ratios and ratios as the structure of the soul, in a line that runs from Nicomachus to the Andalusian theorists.","The Brethren were also stylists, and the twenty-second epistle became one of the most translated pieces of Arabic prose ever written. The animals bring a case against humankind before the king of the jinn: they have been enslaved, eaten, worked and killed, and they wish to know by what right. Delegations of beasts, birds and insects argue against a delegation of men, and the animals have the better of nearly every exchange until the human side is reduced to a single argument about the immortal soul. It is a beast-fable with a genuine philosophical structure, and it circulated in Hebrew, Persian, Turkish and Urdu long before it reached European languages. The fifty-second and last epistle is on magic and talismans, and it is not an appendix bolted on: the curriculum arrives there, having established that the cosmos is one sympathetic body, because that premise is precisely what makes an operation on one part of it act upon another.","Who they were is unresolved and the two accounts should be given together. Abū Ḥayyān al-Tawḥīdī, writing about 983, names a circle at Baṣra — Zayd ibn Rifāʿa, Abū Sulaymān al-Maqdisī, ʿAlī ibn Hārūn al-Zanjānī and others — and his is the earliest external notice. The Ṭayyibī Ismāʿīlī tradition holds instead that the epistles were written by one of the concealed imāms of its own line, and that account is itself a fact about how the corpus was transmitted and by whom it was preserved, since the Ṭayyibī communities of Yemen and Gujarat kept it when others did not. Scholarly dating runs from the late ninth century, argued by Abbas Hamdani from the corpus's fit with the early Ismāʿīlī mission, to the second half of the tenth, argued by S. M. Stern, Yves Marquet and the editors of the Oxford critical edition. The majority holds to about 350–370/960–980, and is under fresh pressure from the redating of the Ghāya, which quotes the Rasāʾil and may be older than they are said to be.","Their reach is out of proportion to their anonymity. They were read from Yemen to al-Andalus — the report that Maslama of Cordoba introduced them to Spain is old, and which Maslama is meant is now part of the Ghāya's authorship problem. The Ghāya quotes them; the lettrists take their number-metaphysics; Ibn Sīnā is said in his autobiography to have been shown them by his father, who inclined to the Ismāʿīlī mission, and to have declined to be persuaded; the later Sufi cosmologies draw on them at points verbatim. Portions crossed into Latin at second hand, through the Picatrix among other routes, centuries before the Arabic was critically edited at all. That edition is now in progress: the Institute of Ismaili Studies has been publishing the epistles with facing translation through Oxford University Press since 2008, which means that for the first time a reader can follow the curriculum in the order its authors built."],terms:[{native:"رسائل",translit:"rasāʾil",gloss:"epistles; the fifty-two treatises, arranged in four parts as a complete curriculum from arithmetic to eschatology"},{native:"إخوان الصفا",translit:"Ikhwān al-Ṣafāʾ",gloss:"the Brethren of Purity; a self-description rather than a name others gave them, and the only name they left"},{native:"العقل الكلي والنفس الكلية",translit:"al-ʿaql al-kullī wa-l-nafs al-kulliyya",gloss:"the Universal Intellect and Universal Soul; the two hypostases below the One, taken from the Arabic Plotinus and made the frame of the whole system"},{native:"العالم الصغير",translit:"al-ʿālam al-ṣaghīr",gloss:"the microcosm; the human being as the small copy of the cosmos, which is why an operation on one may act upon the other"},{native:"ناموس",translit:"nāmūs",gloss:"the revealed law as a divinely given order; from Greek nomos, and used by the Brethren where a jurist would say sharīʿa"},{native:"دور",translit:"dawr",gloss:"cycle; the recurring period of prophecy and concealment in Ismāʿīlī and related cosmology, which gives history a shape rather than a direction"}],texts:[{title:"Rasāʾil Ikhwān al-Ṣafāʾ",note:"Fifty-two epistles in four parts: mathematical, natural, psychological, theological. Being published with facing English translation by the Institute of Ismaili Studies and Oxford University Press since 2008 — the first critical text."},{title:"Epistle 22: The Case of the Animals versus Man before the King of the Jinn",note:"The beast-fable in which the animals argue their case and very nearly win it; translated into Hebrew, Persian, Turkish and Urdu long before it reached Europe."},{title:"Epistle 52: on magic and talismans",note:"The last treatise of the curriculum, and its intended destination rather than an appendix — the operation follows from the doctrine of a single sympathetic cosmos established in the preceding fifty-one."},{title:"Abū Ḥayyān al-Tawḥīdī, al-Imtāʿ wa-l-muʾānasa",note:"Written about 983; names a circle at Baṣra and is the earliest external notice of the authors. Set against it stands the Ṭayyibī Ismāʿīlī account attributing the epistles to a concealed imām."},{title:"Abbas Hamdani and S. M. Stern on the dating",note:"Hamdani argues for the late ninth century from the corpus's fit with the early daʿwa; Stern, Marquet and the Oxford editors for the second half of the tenth. The redating of the Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm has lately revived the earlier case."}]},{slug:"jafr-and-divination",title:"Jafr and the Divinatory Sciences",native:"الجفر",translit:"al-jafr",summary:"A parchment said to hold everything that will happen, a lettered wheel that answers questions, sixteen figures made of dots, and a state astrologer on the Ottoman payroll until the end.",paragraphs:["Jafr begins as an object. Early Imāmī tradition — the reports are gathered in al-Kulaynī's al-Kāfī, in the Book of the Proof — holds that the imāms inherited certain writings from the Prophet through ʿAlī: a ṣaḥīfa, a book called al-Jāmiʿa, and al-Jafr, said to be a skin or parchment containing the knowledge of what has been and what will be until the resurrection. The word jafr means a young goat, hence the hide. Whatever these reports originally described, by the medieval period jafr names something else entirely: a technique for extracting prognostication from letters and numbers, worked especially on the disconnected letters of the Qurʾān, and applied above all to the rise and fall of dynasties. Ibn Khaldūn discusses the Kitāb al-Jafr circulating under Jaʿfar al-Ṣādiq's name in his own day and reports that nothing certain is known of it — the tradition's own scepticism, from a man who did not doubt that several of these sciences work.","What jafr was actually used for is politics. A dynasty's name, or the letters of a Qurʾānic verse, computed and recombined, yields a date and a name; and because the technique can be worked in either direction it can as easily confirm a ruler's legitimacy as predict his fall. The clearest case is the Shajara al-nuʿmāniyya, a prophetic text ascribed to Ibn al-ʿArabī — pseudepigraphically, on any reckoning — which was read as foretelling the Ottoman conquest of Syria, and which circulated to good effect around Selim I's entry into Damascus in 1518 and his restoration of Ibn al-ʿArabī's tomb. Apocalyptic jafr computation intensified around the Islamic millennium of 1000 AH / 1591–2 CE, in Ottoman, Safavid and Mughal chanceries alike, which is one reason the period looks in the manuscripts less like the decline European historiography long assumed and more like what Matthew Melvin-Koushki calls an occult renaissance.","The zāʾirja is the most elaborate machine the tradition built. It is a great chart of concentric rings bearing the letters of the alphabet, the signs, the elements and numerical series, traditionally credited to Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Sabtī of Marrakesh (d. 601/1204), and it is operated by a fixed procedure: a question is posed in words, its letters are taken with the ascendant degree at the moment of asking, and a sequence of transpositions, multiplications and substitutions delivers a versified answer. Ibn Khaldūn prints the chart in the Muqaddima and works an example through it, and his verdict is the interesting part — he treats the operation as a genuine technical craft whose results follow from the rules, and he declines to say that it discloses the future, since the correspondence between the letters and the events remains unestablished. That is a piece of methodological criticism unusually clean for its century.","Alongside the lettered sciences run the older ones. ʿIlm al-raml, the science of the sand, generates sixteen figures from rows of dots made at random, distributes them among twelve houses on the model of an astrological chart, and derives further figures from those by fixed combination; it is attributed traditionally to Idrīs or to Daniel, its surviving Arabic treatises begin in the twelfth and thirteenth centuries, and the widely printed handbook goes under the name of al-Zanātī. It crossed into Latin as geomantia, into the Sahel and East Africa, and to Madagascar as sikidy, where the relationship is demonstrable; its relation to Yoruba Ifá is argued and unsettled. Dream interpretation had a large literature of its own — the earliest substantial Arabic dream-book to survive is al-Dīnawarī's, written about 397/1006 — and was treated with more indulgence than the rest, because a hadith in Bukhārī makes the true dream one part of forty-six of prophecy. The books that go under Ibn Sīrīn's name are later than the eighth-century interpreter they honour.","The legal frontier here is sharper than in the other currents, and it runs through a single distinction. Istikhāra — the prayer for guidance, taught by the Prophet according to a hadith of Jābir in Bukhārī, in which one asks God to make a matter easy or turn it away — is an ordinary devotion performed by ordinary Muslims today, and it is not divination, because it asks rather than reads. Everything that claims to read is at risk: the Qurʾān forbids the arrows of the pre-Islamic diviners (Q 5:3, Q 5:90), and hadith condemn the kāhin, so that jurists who allow amulets will still refuse the fortune-teller. And yet astrology was a salaried state function. The Ottoman court maintained a chief astrologer, the müneccimbaşı, who elected the hours for enthronements, campaigns and the laying of foundations, and the office existed from the fifteenth century until the empire did not. Ibn Khaldūn condemned judicial astrology; Sharaf al-Dīn Yazdī wrote a defence of geomancy answering him. The argument was never won, and geomancers, dream-interpreters and amulet-writers are working in Cairo, Fez and Karachi this morning, contested exactly as they were in the fourteenth century."],terms:[{native:"جفر",translit:"jafr",gloss:"a young goat, hence a parchment; the inherited book of the imāms in early Shīʿī tradition, and later the name of a technique of letter-prognostication applied to dynasties"},{native:"زايرجة",translit:"zāʾirja",gloss:"the lettered wheel of concentric rings that converts a question and an ascendant into a versified answer; Ibn Khaldūn prints it and works an example, calling it a craft and not a disclosure"},{native:"علم الرمل",translit:"ʿilm al-raml",gloss:"geomancy, the science of the sand; sixteen figures generated from rows of dots and read in twelve houses. Latin geomantia, Malagasy sikidy"},{native:"استخارة",translit:"istikhāra",gloss:"the prayer for guidance, taught in a hadith of Jābir in Bukhārī; a request rather than a reading, which is why it stands on the licit side of a line that most of this current's subjects cross"},{native:"فأل",translit:"faʾl",gloss:"an omen taken from a chance word, sign or opening of a book; treated indulgently when it is good and condemned as ṭiyara when it is read as ill-boding"},{native:"أحكام النجوم",translit:"aḥkām al-nujūm",gloss:"judicial astrology, the drawing of judgements from the stars; condemned by Ibn Khaldūn and by many jurists, and practised at every court that could afford an astrologer"}],texts:[{title:"al-Kulaynī, al-Kāfī, Kitāb al-Ḥujja",note:"The early Imāmī reports on the writings the imāms are held to have inherited — the ṣaḥīfa, al-Jāmiʿa and al-Jafr — from which the whole later science takes its name and its warrant."},{title:"Ibn Khaldūn, Muqaddima, on the zāʾirja",note:"The chart printed, an example worked through, and the conclusion that the procedure is a technical craft whose rules produce its results — with the correspondence to future events left unproved."},{title:"Pseudo-al-Zanātī, Kitāb al-faṣl fī uṣūl ʿilm al-raml",note:"The most widely printed Arabic geomancy. The attribution is obscure and the printed recensions late, so the book is evidence for the practice rather than for its origins."},{title:"Abū Saʿd al-Dīnawarī, al-Qādirī fī al-taʿbīr",note:"Written about 397/1006 for a vizier; the earliest substantial Arabic dream-book to survive complete. The works circulating under Ibn Sīrīn's name are considerably later than he is."},{title:"Matthew Melvin-Koushki, 'In Defense of Geomancy: Šaraf al-Dīn Yazdī Rebuts Ibn Ḫaldūn's Critique of the Occult Sciences', Arabica 64 (2017)",note:"A fifteenth-century answer to the Muqaddima, and the case that the post-Mongol centuries were an expansion of these sciences rather than their decline."}]}]};export{e as default};
